    {
      "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-19",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "19 Persons imprisoned under Australian law",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995#sec-19"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995.pdf, p.28",
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Attorney-General must not issue a surrender warrant if:\n(a) the person is serving a sentence of imprisonment in respect of\nan offence against an Australian law, or is otherwise subject\nto detention under an Australian law; and\n(b) the Tribunal has been required to give adequate undertakings\nto the Attorney-General relating to:\n(i) the person’s return to Australia to serve the remainder\nof the sentence or other detention once the person is no\nlonger required to be detained by, or on the order of, the\nTribunal; and\n(ii) the person’s custody while travelling, and while in other\ncountries, for the Tribunal’s purposes; and\n(c) the Attorney-General is not satisfied that the Tribunal has\ngiven adequate undertakings relating to those matters.\n(2) For the purposes of this section, the person is not taken to be\nserving a sentence of imprisonment, or to be otherwise subject to\ndetention, if he or she has been released on parole or licence, or has\nbeen otherwise conditionally released, for the remainder of the\nsentence or period of detention.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) The Attorney-General must not issue a surrender warrant if:\n(a) the person is serving a sentence of imprisonment in respect of\nan offence against an Australian law, or is otherwise subject\nto detention under an Australian law; and\n(b) the Tribunal has been required to give adequate undertakings\nto the Attorney-General relating to:\n(i) the person’s return to Australia to serve the remainder\nof the sentence or other detention once the person is no\nlonger required to be detained by, or on the order of, the\nTribunal; and\n(ii) the person’s custody while travelling, and while in other\ncountries, for the Tribunal’s purposes; and\n(c) the Attorney-General is not satisfied that the Tribunal has\ngiven adequate undertakings relating to those matters.\n(2) For the purposes of this section, the person is not taken to be\nserving a sentence of imprisonment, or to be otherwise subject to\ndetention, if he or she has been released on parole or licence, or has\nbeen otherwise conditionally released, for the remainder of the\nsentence or period of detention.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-19-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-19-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "a34958380688f8d34a1a4cfab3fc3e86e706ecddda2c7539b01e7e8da8d277f5"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995#sec-19",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "19 Persons imprisoned under Australian law",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 28,
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Attorney-General must not issue a surrender warrant if:\n(a) the person is serving a sentence of imprisonment in respect of\nan offence against an Australian law, or is otherwise subject\nto detention under an Australian law; and\n(b) the Tribunal has been required to give adequate undertakings\nto the Attorney-General relating to:\n(i) the person’s return to Australia to serve the remainder\nof the sentence or other detention once the person is no\nlonger required to be detained by, or on the order of, the\nTribunal; and\n(ii) the person’s custody while travelling, and while in other\ncountries, for the Tribunal’s purposes; and\n(c) the Attorney-General is not satisfied that the Tribunal has\ngiven adequate undertakings relating to those matters.\n(2) For the purposes of this section, the person is not taken to be\nserving a sentence of imprisonment, or to be otherwise subject to\ndetention, if he or she has been released on parole or licence, or has\nbeen otherwise conditionally released, for the remainder of the\nsentence or period of detention."
    },
