    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-32b",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "32B Authorising application for carrying out of forensic procedures",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995#sec-32b"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995.pdf, p.40",
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Attorney-General may authorise, in writing, a constable to\napply under Part ID of the Crimes Act 1914 for an order for the\ncarrying out of a forensic procedure on a person if:\n(a) a Tribunal has requested the procedure to be carried out on\nthe person; and\n(b) the Attorney-General is satisfied:\n(i) that the request relates to a proceeding before, or an\ninvestigation conducted by, the Tribunal; and\n(ii) that the person is, or is believed to be, in Australia; and\n(iii) that the Tribunal has given appropriate undertakings\nabout the retention, use and destruction of forensic\nmaterial, or of information obtained from analysing that\nmaterial; and\n(iv) that the Tribunal has given any other undertakings that\nthe Attorney-General considers necessary; and\n(v) unless subsection (2) applies—that the person has been\ngiven an opportunity to consent to the forensic\nprocedure and has not consented to it; and\n(vi) if subsection (2) applies—of the matters in that\nsubsection; and\n(c) in the case of the person being a suspect, the constable is an\nauthorised applicant.\n(2) If the person is a child or an incapable person, the matters are:\n(a) that either:\n(i) the consent of a parent or guardian of the person cannot\nreasonably be obtained or has been withdrawn; or\n(ii) a parent or guardian of the person is a suspect in relation\nto an offence to which the investigation or proceeding\nrelates; and\n\n\nOther forms of assistance to a Tribunal  Part 4\nForensic procedures  Division 1B\nSection 32C\nInternational War Crimes Tribunals Act 1995\nCompilation date: 28/03/2026\n(b) that, having regard to the best interests of the person, it is\nappropriate to make the authorisation.\n(3) In this section:\nauthorised applicant has the same meaning as in\nsubsection 23WA(1) of the Crimes Act 1914.\nsuspect has the same meaning as in subsection 23WA(1) of the\nCrimes Act 1914.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) The Attorney-General may authorise, in writing, a constable to\napply under Part ID of the Crimes Act 1914 for an order for the\ncarrying out of a forensic procedure on a person if:\n(a) a Tribunal has requested the procedure to be carried out on\nthe person; and\n(b) the Attorney-General is satisfied:\n(i) that the request relates to a proceeding before, or an\ninvestigation conducted by, the Tribunal; and\n(ii) that the person is, or is believed to be, in Australia; and\n(iii) that the Tribunal has given appropriate undertakings\nabout the retention, use and destruction of forensic\nmaterial, or of information obtained from analysing that\nmaterial; and\n(iv) that the Tribunal has given any other undertakings that\nthe Attorney-General considers necessary; and\n(v) unless subsection (2) applies—that the person has been\ngiven an opportunity to consent to the forensic\nprocedure and has not consented to it; and\n(vi) if subsection (2) applies—of the matters in that\nsubsection; and\n(c) in the case of the person being a suspect, the constable is an\nauthorised applicant.\n(2) If the person is a child or an incapable person, the matters are:\n(a) that either:\n(i) the consent of a parent or guardian of the person cannot\nreasonably be obtained or has been withdrawn; or\n(ii) a parent or guardian of the person is a suspect in relation\nto an offence to which the investigation or proceeding\nrelates; and\n\n\nOther forms of assistance to a Tribunal  Part 4\nForensic procedures  Division 1B\nSection 32C\nInternational War Crimes Tribunals Act 1995\nCompilation date: 28/03/2026\n(b) that, having regard to the best interests of the person, it is\nappropriate to make the authorisation.\n(3) In this section:\nauthorised applicant has the same meaning as in\nsubsection 23WA(1) of the Crimes Act 1914.\nsuspect has the same meaning as in subsection 23WA(1) of the\nCrimes Act 1914.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-32b-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-32b-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-32b-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "b744715fa9d419cf15df4cd337b6a2cf10226e46591d643b927254da8b46723b"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995#sec-32b",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "32B Authorising application for carrying out of forensic procedures",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 40,
      "values:originalText": "(1) The Attorney-General may authorise, in writing, a constable to\napply under Part ID of the Crimes Act 1914 for an order for the\ncarrying out of a forensic procedure on a person if:\n(a) a Tribunal has requested the procedure to be carried out on\nthe person; and\n(b) the Attorney-General is satisfied:\n(i) that the request relates to a proceeding before, or an\ninvestigation conducted by, the Tribunal; and\n(ii) that the person is, or is believed to be, in Australia; and\n(iii) that the Tribunal has given appropriate undertakings\nabout the retention, use and destruction of forensic\nmaterial, or of information obtained from analysing that\nmaterial; and\n(iv) that the Tribunal has given any other undertakings that\nthe Attorney-General considers necessary; and\n(v) unless subsection (2) applies—that the person has been\ngiven an opportunity to consent to the forensic\nprocedure and has not consented to it; and\n(vi) if subsection (2) applies—of the matters in that\nsubsection; and\n(c) in the case of the person being a suspect, the constable is an\nauthorised applicant.\n(2) If the person is a child or an incapable person, the matters are:\n(a) that either:\n(i) the consent of a parent or guardian of the person cannot\nreasonably be obtained or has been withdrawn; or\n(ii) a parent or guardian of the person is a suspect in relation\nto an offence to which the investigation or proceeding\nrelates; and\n\n\nOther forms of assistance to a Tribunal  Part 4\nForensic procedures  Division 1B\nSection 32C\nInternational War Crimes Tribunals Act 1995\nCompilation date: 28/03/2026\n(b) that, having regard to the best interests of the person, it is\nappropriate to make the authorisation.\n(3) In this section:\nauthorised applicant has the same meaning as in\nsubsection 23WA(1) of the Crimes Act 1914.\nsuspect has the same meaning as in subsection 23WA(1) of the\nCrimes Act 1914."
    },
