    {
      "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-33",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "33 Attorney-General may authorise applications for search",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995#sec-33"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995.pdf, p.42",
      "values:originalText": "warrants\n(1) Subject to subsection (2), if:\n(a) a Tribunal makes a request to the Attorney-General\ncompliance with which may involve the issue of a search\nwarrant in relation to evidential material; and\n(b) there are reasonable grounds to believe that the material is in\nAustralia;\nthe Attorney-General may, in writing, authorise a police officer to\napply to a magistrate of the State or Territory in which that\nmaterial is believed to be located for the search warrant.\n(2) The Attorney-General must not comply with the request if, in his\nor her opinion:\n(a) complying with the request would prejudice Australia’s\nsovereignty, security or national interest; or\n(b) there are special circumstances justifying non-compliance.",
      "skos:definition": "warrants\n(1) Subject to subsection (2), if:\n(a) a Tribunal makes a request to the Attorney-General\ncompliance with which may involve the issue of a search\nwarrant in relation to evidential material; and\n(b) there are reasonable grounds to believe that the material is in\nAustralia;\nthe Attorney-General may, in writing, authorise a police officer to\napply to a magistrate of the State or Territory in which that\nmaterial is believed to be located for the search warrant.\n(2) The Attorney-General must not comply with the request if, in his\nor her opinion:\n(a) complying with the request would prejudice Australia’s\nsovereignty, security or national interest; or\n(b) there are special circumstances justifying non-compliance.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-33-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-33-ss-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "6c4309c341e96053f5d26ad988867a9fd0938b074f54360d3e3a1ef12b1377fb"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995#sec-33",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "33 Attorney-General may authorise applications for search",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 42,
      "values:originalText": "warrants\n(1) Subject to subsection (2), if:\n(a) a Tribunal makes a request to the Attorney-General\ncompliance with which may involve the issue of a search\nwarrant in relation to evidential material; and\n(b) there are reasonable grounds to believe that the material is in\nAustralia;\nthe Attorney-General may, in writing, authorise a police officer to\napply to a magistrate of the State or Territory in which that\nmaterial is believed to be located for the search warrant.\n(2) The Attorney-General must not comply with the request if, in his\nor her opinion:\n(a) complying with the request would prejudice Australia’s\nsovereignty, security or national interest; or\n(b) there are special circumstances justifying non-compliance."
    },
    {
