    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-34",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "34 Applications for search warrants",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995#sec-34"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995.pdf, p.42",
      "values:originalText": "(1) If:\n(a) a police officer is authorised under section 33 to apply for a\nsearch warrant; and\n(b) the police officer has reasonable grounds for suspecting that\nthe evidential material is, or within the applicable period\nreferred to in subsection (3) of this section will be, at any\npremises;\nthe police officer may, by an information on oath setting out the\ngrounds for that suspicion, apply for a search warrant in relation to\nthe premises to search for that material.\n(2) If:\n\n\nOther forms of assistance to a Tribunal  Part 4\nSearch and seizure  Division 2\nSection 34\nInternational War Crimes Tribunals Act 1995\nCompilation date: 28/03/2026\n(a) a police officer is authorised under section 33 to apply for a\nsearch warrant; and\n(b) the police officer has reasonable grounds for suspecting that\nthe evidential material is, or within the applicable period\nreferred to in subsection (3) of this section will be, in a\nperson’s possession;\nthe police officer may, by an information on oath setting out the\ngrounds for that suspicion, apply for a search warrant in relation to\nthat person to search for that material.\n(3) For the purposes of this section, the applicable period is:\n(a) if the application for the warrant is made by telephone, telex,\nfax or other electronic means, as provided by section 52-48\nhours; or\n(b) otherwise-72 hours.\nNote:\nPart 7 deals with search warrants.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) If:\n(a) a police officer is authorised under section 33 to apply for a\nsearch warrant; and\n(b) the police officer has reasonable grounds for suspecting that\nthe evidential material is, or within the applicable period\nreferred to in subsection (3) of this section will be, at any\npremises;\nthe police officer may, by an information on oath setting out the\ngrounds for that suspicion, apply for a search warrant in relation to\nthe premises to search for that material.\n(2) If:\n\n\nOther forms of assistance to a Tribunal  Part 4\nSearch and seizure  Division 2\nSection 34\nInternational War Crimes Tribunals Act 1995\nCompilation date: 28/03/2026\n(a) a police officer is authorised under section 33 to apply for a\nsearch warrant; and\n(b) the police officer has reasonable grounds for suspecting that\nthe evidential material is, or within the applicable period\nreferred to in subsection (3) of this section will be, in a\nperson’s possession;\nthe police officer may, by an information on oath setting out the\ngrounds for that suspicion, apply for a search warrant in relation to\nthat person to search for that material.\n(3) For the purposes of this section, the applicable period is:\n(a) if the application for the warrant is made by telephone, telex,\nfax or other electronic means, as provided by section 52-48\nhours; or\n(b) otherwise-72 hours.\nNote:\nPart 7 deals with search warrants.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-34-ss-1"
