      "cml:conclusionText": "Person continues to serve that sentence of imprisonment AND person is subject to detention under Commonwealth law"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-39",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "39 Transit",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995#sec-39"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995.pdf, p.50",
      "values:originalText": "(1) If a person is to be transported in custody from a foreign country\nthrough Australia to another foreign country for the purpose of:\n(a) giving evidence in a proceeding before a Tribunal; or\n(b) giving assistance in relation to an investigation being\nconducted by a Tribunal; or\n(c) being surrendered to a Tribunal;\nthe person may be transported through Australia in the custody of\nanother person.\n(2) If an aircraft or ship by which the person is being transported lands\nor calls at a place in Australia, the person must be kept in such\ncustody as the Attorney-General directs in writing until his or her\ntransportation is continued.\n(3) If:\n(a) a person is being held in custody pursuant to a direction\nunder subsection (2); and\n(b) the person’s transportation is not, in the Attorney-General’s\nopinion, continued within a reasonable time;\nthe Attorney-General may direct that the person be transported in\ncustody to the foreign country from which the person was first\ntransported.\n\n\nOther forms of assistance to a Tribunal  Part 4\nService of process  Division 5\nSection 40\nInternational War Crimes Tribunals Act 1995\nCompilation date: 28/03/2026",
      "skos:definition": "(1) If a person is to be transported in custody from a foreign country\nthrough Australia to another foreign country for the purpose of:\n(a) giving evidence in a proceeding before a Tribunal; or\n(b) giving assistance in relation to an investigation being\nconducted by a Tribunal; or\n(c) being surrendered to a Tribunal;\nthe person may be transported through Australia in the custody of\nanother person.\n(2) If an aircraft or ship by which the person is being transported lands\nor calls at a place in Australia, the person must be kept in such\ncustody as the Attorney-General directs in writing until his or her\ntransportation is continued.\n(3) If:\n(a) a person is being held in custody pursuant to a direction\nunder subsection (2); and\n(b) the person’s transportation is not, in the Attorney-General’s\nopinion, continued within a reasonable time;\nthe Attorney-General may direct that the person be transported in\ncustody to the foreign country from which the person was first\ntransported.\n\n\nOther forms of assistance to a Tribunal  Part 4\nService of process  Division 5\nSection 40\nInternational War Crimes Tribunals Act 1995\nCompilation date: 28/03/2026",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-39-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-39-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-39-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "3743285caf230b5e083b2f1aa9c82b4806974353a8e23f9b9ed281e1cf20f24f"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995#sec-39",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "39 Transit",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 50,
      "values:originalText": "(1) If a person is to be transported in custody from a foreign country\nthrough Australia to another foreign country for the purpose of:\n(a) giving evidence in a proceeding before a Tribunal; or\n(b) giving assistance in relation to an investigation being\nconducted by a Tribunal; or\n(c) being surrendered to a Tribunal;\nthe person may be transported through Australia in the custody of\nanother person.\n(2) If an aircraft or ship by which the person is being transported lands\nor calls at a place in Australia, the person must be kept in such\ncustody as the Attorney-General directs in writing until his or her\ntransportation is continued.\n(3) If:\n(a) a person is being held in custody pursuant to a direction\nunder subsection (2); and\n(b) the person’s transportation is not, in the Attorney-General’s\nopinion, continued within a reasonable time;\nthe Attorney-General may direct that the person be transported in\ncustody to the foreign country from which the person was first\ntransported.\n\n\nOther forms of assistance to a Tribunal  Part 4\nService of process  Division 5\nSection 40\nInternational War Crimes Tribunals Act 1995\nCompilation date: 28/03/2026"
    },
