    {
      "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-42",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "42 Tribunal’s powers while sitting in Australia",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995#sec-42"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995.pdf, p.66",
      "values:originalText": "(1) While a Tribunal is sitting in Australia, it may exercise such\npowers as are prescribed by the regulations in respect of the\nTribunal.\n(2) Regulations made for the purposes of subsection (1) must\nimplement agreements between the Commonwealth and the\nTribunal about the powers that the Tribunal may exercise while\nsitting in Australia.\n(3) Despite section 14 of the Legislation Act 2003, regulations made\nfor the purposes of subsection (1) may apply, adopt or incorporate,\nwith or without modification, the rules of procedure and evidence,\nas in force at a particular time or as in force from time to time,\nadopted under:\n(a) in the case of the Former Yugoslavia Tribunal—Article 15 of\nthe Statute of the Tribunal; or\n(b) in the case of the Rwanda Tribunal—Article 14 of the Statute\nof the Tribunal; or\n\n\nSittings of a Tribunal in Australia  Part 5\n\nSection 43\nInternational War Crimes Tribunals Act 1995\nCompilation date: 28/03/2026\n(c) in the case of the International Residual Mechanism for\nCriminal Tribunals—Article 13 of the Statute of the\nTribunal.\n43  Contravention of Tribunal orders etc.\nA person must not:\n(a) intentionally contravene an order that a Tribunal makes while\nsitting in Australia; or\n(b) otherwise intentionally hinder a Tribunal in performing its\nfunctions while sitting in Australia.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 2 years.\nNote:\nUnder subsection 4D(1) of the Crimes Act 1914, this penalty is only a\nmaximum penalty for the offence.  Subsection 4B(2) of that Act\nallows a court to impose an appropriate fine instead of, or in addition\nto, a term of imprisonment. If a body corporate is convicted of the\noffence, subsection 4B(3) of that Act allows a court to impose a fine\nof an amount not greater than 5 times the maximum fine that the court\ncould impose on an individual convicted of the same offence.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) While a Tribunal is sitting in Australia, it may exercise such\npowers as are prescribed by the regulations in respect of the\nTribunal.\n(2) Regulations made for the purposes of subsection (1) must\nimplement agreements between the Commonwealth and the\nTribunal about the powers that the Tribunal may exercise while\nsitting in Australia.\n(3) Despite section 14 of the Legislation Act 2003, regulations made\nfor the purposes of subsection (1) may apply, adopt or incorporate,\nwith or without modification, the rules of procedure and evidence,\nas in force at a particular time or as in force from time to time,\nadopted under:\n(a) in the case of the Former Yugoslavia Tribunal—Article 15 of\nthe Statute of the Tribunal; or\n(b) in the case of the Rwanda Tribunal—Article 14 of the Statute\nof the Tribunal; or\n\n\nSittings of a Tribunal in Australia  Part 5\n\nSection 43\nInternational War Crimes Tribunals Act 1995\nCompilation date: 28/03/2026\n(c) in the case of the International Residual Mechanism for\nCriminal Tribunals—Article 13 of the Statute of the\nTribunal.\n43  Contravention of Tribunal orders etc.\nA person must not:\n(a) intentionally contravene an order that a Tribunal makes while\nsitting in Australia; or\n(b) otherwise intentionally hinder a Tribunal in performing its\nfunctions while sitting in Australia.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 2 years.\nNote:\nUnder subsection 4D(1) of the Crimes Act 1914, this penalty is only a\nmaximum penalty for the offence.  Subsection 4B(2) of that Act\nallows a court to impose an appropriate fine instead of, or in addition\nto, a term of imprisonment. If a body corporate is convicted of the\noffence, subsection 4B(3) of that Act allows a court to impose a fine\nof an amount not greater than 5 times the maximum fine that the court\ncould impose on an individual convicted of the same offence.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-42-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-42-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-42-ss-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "22fb89f51b0ec9907ce1ad1ba829b26ca33f75a1390e6043263d1d16e54b2040"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995#sec-42",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "42 Tribunal’s powers while sitting in Australia",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 66,
      "values:originalText": "(1) While a Tribunal is sitting in Australia, it may exercise such\npowers as are prescribed by the regulations in respect of the\nTribunal.\n(2) Regulations made for the purposes of subsection (1) must\nimplement agreements between the Commonwealth and the\nTribunal about the powers that the Tribunal may exercise while\nsitting in Australia.\n(3) Despite section 14 of the Legislation Act 2003, regulations made\nfor the purposes of subsection (1) may apply, adopt or incorporate,\nwith or without modification, the rules of procedure and evidence,\nas in force at a particular time or as in force from time to time,\nadopted under:\n(a) in the case of the Former Yugoslavia Tribunal—Article 15 of\nthe Statute of the Tribunal; or\n(b) in the case of the Rwanda Tribunal—Article 14 of the Statute\nof the Tribunal; or\n\n\nSittings of a Tribunal in Australia  Part 5\n\nSection 43\nInternational War Crimes Tribunals Act 1995\nCompilation date: 28/03/2026\n(c) in the case of the International Residual Mechanism for\nCriminal Tribunals—Article 13 of the Statute of the\nTribunal.\n43  Contravention of Tribunal orders etc.\nA person must not:\n(a) intentionally contravene an order that a Tribunal makes while\nsitting in Australia; or\n(b) otherwise intentionally hinder a Tribunal in performing its\nfunctions while sitting in Australia.\nPenalty: Imprisonment for 2 years.\nNote:\nUnder subsection 4D(1) of the Crimes Act 1914, this penalty is only a\nmaximum penalty for the offence.  Subsection 4B(2) of that Act\nallows a court to impose an appropriate fine instead of, or in addition\nto, a term of imprisonment. If a body corporate is convicted of the\noffence, subsection 4B(3) of that Act allows a court to impose a fine\nof an amount not greater than 5 times the maximum fine that the court\ncould impose on an individual convicted of the same offence."
    },
