    {
      "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-51",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "51 Restrictions on personal searches",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995#sec-51"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995.pdf, p.78",
      "values:originalText": "A warrant cannot authorise a strip search or a search of a person’s\nbody cavities.\n52  Warrants may be issued by telephone etc.\n(1) A police officer may apply to a magistrate for a warrant by\ntelephone, telex, fax or other electronic means:\n(a) in an urgent case; or\n(b) if the delay that would occur if an application were made in\nperson would frustrate the effective execution of the warrant.\n(2) The magistrate may require communication by voice to the extent\nthat is practicable in the circumstances.\n(3) An application under this section must include all information\nrequired to be provided in an ordinary application for a warrant,\nbut the application may, if necessary, be made before the\ninformation is sworn.\n(4) If an application is made to a magistrate under this section and the\nmagistrate, after considering the information and having received\nand considered such further information (if any) as the magistrate\nrequires, is satisfied that:\n\n\nSearch, seizure and powers of arrest  Part 7\nSearch warrants  Division 1\nSection 53\nInternational War Crimes Tribunals Act 1995\nCompilation date: 28/03/2026\n(a) a warrant in the terms of the application should be issued\nurgently; or\n(b) the delay that would occur if an application were made in\nperson would frustrate the effective execution of the warrant;\nthe magistrate may complete and sign the same form of warrant\nthat would be issued under section 47.\n53  Formalities relating to warrants issued by telephone etc.\n(1) If the magistrate decides to issue the warrant under section 52, the\nmagistrate is to inform the applicant, by telephone, telex, fax or\nother electronic means, of the terms of the warrant and the day on\nwhich and the time at which it was signed.\n(2) The applicant must then complete a form of warrant in terms\nsubstantially corresponding to those given by the magistrate,\nstating on the form the name of the magistrate and the day on\nwhich and the time at which the warrant was signed.\n(3) The applicant must, not later than the day after the day of expiry of\nthe warrant or the day after the day on which the warrant was\nexecuted, whichever is the earlier, give or transmit to the\nmagistrate:\n(a) the form of warrant completed by the applicant; and\n(b) if the information referred to in subsection 52(3) was not\nsworn—that information duly sworn.\n(4) The magistrate is to attach to the documents provided under\nsubsection (3) the form of warrant completed by the magistrate.\n(5) If:\n(a) it is material, in any proceedings, for a court to be satisfied\nthat the exercise of a power under a warrant issued under\nsection 52 was duly authorised; and\n(b) the form of warrant signed by the magistrate is not produced\nin evidence;\nthe court is to assume, unless the contrary is proved, that the\nexercise of the power was not duly authorised.",
      "skos:definition": "A warrant cannot authorise a strip search or a search of a person’s\nbody cavities.\n52  Warrants may be issued by telephone etc.\n(1) A police officer may apply to a magistrate for a warrant by\ntelephone, telex, fax or other electronic means:\n(a) in an urgent case; or\n(b) if the delay that would occur if an application were made in\nperson would frustrate the effective execution of the warrant.\n(2) The magistrate may require communication by voice to the extent\nthat is practicable in the circumstances.\n(3) An application under this section must include all information\nrequired to be provided in an ordinary application for a warrant,\nbut the application may, if necessary, be made before the\ninformation is sworn.\n(4) If an application is made to a magistrate under this section and the\nmagistrate, after considering the information and having received\nand considered such further information (if any) as the magistrate\nrequires, is satisfied that:\n\n\nSearch, seizure and powers of arrest  Part 7\nSearch warrants  Division 1\nSection 53\nInternational War Crimes Tribunals Act 1995\nCompilation date: 28/03/2026\n(a) a warrant in the terms of the application should be issued\nurgently; or\n(b) the delay that would occur if an application were made in\nperson would frustrate the effective execution of the warrant;\nthe magistrate may complete and sign the same form of warrant\nthat would be issued under section 47.\n53  Formalities relating to warrants issued by telephone etc.\n(1) If the magistrate decides to issue the warrant under section 52, the\nmagistrate is to inform the applicant, by telephone, telex, fax or\nother electronic means, of the terms of the warrant and the day on\nwhich and the time at which it was signed.\n(2) The applicant must then complete a form of warrant in terms\nsubstantially corresponding to those given by the magistrate,\nstating on the form the name of the magistrate and the day on\nwhich and the time at which the warrant was signed.\n(3) The applicant must, not later ",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-51-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-51-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-51-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-51-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-51-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-51-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-51-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-51-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-51-ss-5"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "9c63be6c340581a0a0e6dd30ef6ef918a706a08c2c6360c1b6ee97358814288f"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995#sec-51",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "51 Restrictions on personal searches",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 78,
      "values:originalText": "A warrant cannot authorise a strip search or a search of a person’s\nbody cavities.\n52  Warrants may be issued by telephone etc.\n(1) A police officer may apply to a magistrate for a warrant by\ntelephone, telex, fax or other electronic means:\n(a) in an urgent case; or\n(b) if the delay that would occur if an application were made in\nperson would frustrate the effective execution of the warrant.\n(2) The magistrate may require communication by voice to the extent\nthat is practicable in the circumstances.\n(3) An application under this section must include all information\nrequired to be provided in an ordinary application for a warrant,\nbut the application may, if necessary, be made before the\ninformation is sworn.\n(4) If an application is made to a magistrate under this section and the\nmagistrate, after considering the information and having received\nand considered such further information (if any) as the magistrate\nrequires, is satisfied that:\n\n\nSearch, seizure and powers of arrest  Part 7\nSearch warrants  Division 1\nSection 53\nInternational War Crimes Tribunals Act 1995\nCompilation date: 28/03/2026\n(a) a warrant in the terms of the application should be issued\nurgently; or\n(b) the delay that would occur if an application were made in\nperson would frustrate the effective execution of the warrant;\nthe magistrate may complete and sign the same form of warrant\nthat would be issued under section 47.\n53  Formalities relating to warrants issued by telephone etc.\n(1) If the magistrate decides to issue the warrant under section 52, the\nmagistrate is to inform the applicant, by telephone, telex, fax or\nother electronic means, of the terms of the warrant and the day on\nwhich and the time at which it was signed.\n(2) The applicant must then complete a form of warrant in terms\nsubstantially corresponding to those given by the magistrate,\nstating on the form the name of the magistrate and the day on\nwhich and the time at which the warrant was signed.\n(3) The applicant must, not later than the day after the day of expiry of\nthe warrant or the day after the day on which the warrant was\nexecuted, whichever is the earlier, give or transmit to the\nmagistrate:\n(a) the form of warrant completed by the applicant; and\n(b) if the information referred to in subsection 52(3) was not\nsworn—that information duly sworn.\n(4) The magistrate is to attach to the documents provided under\nsubsection (3) the form of warrant completed by the magistrate.\n(5) If:\n(a) it is material, in any proceedings, for a court to be satisfied\nthat the exercise of a power under a warrant issued under\nsection 52 was duly authorised; and\n(b) the form of warrant signed by the magistrate is not produced\nin evidence;\nthe court is to assume, unless the contrary is proved, that the\nexercise of the power was not duly authorised."
    },
    {
