        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-57-ss-2-logic-alleninterval-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-57-ss-2-logic-ltl-3"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "c05459cbd38208fc8e1219bc732cb4f7886f50ca235a13379aa5e763990dd403",
      "skos:note": "Consent of occupier required for examination or processing at premises"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995#sec-57-ss-2",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "57(2) Use of equipment to examine or process things",
      "values:kind": "subsection",
      "cof:pageNumber": 81,
      "values:originalText": "(2) If:\n(a) it is not practicable to examine or process the things at the\nwarrant premises; or\n(b) the occupier of the premises consents in writing;"
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-57-ss-2-norm",
      "@type": "values:Permission",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "values:partOf": {
        "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-57-ss-2"
      },
      "values:deonticStatus": {
        "@id": "values:HeuristicDerived"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "values:borneBy": {
        "@id": "values:Agent"
      }
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-57-ss-2-logic-deontic-1",
      "@type": "cml:LogicApplication",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:Deontic"
      },
      "cml:operator": "Permit",
      "cml:logicSummary": "Permission granted by occupier",
      "cml:executionSurface": "modalities::logic::deontic",
      "cml:confidence": {
        "@value": 1.0,
        "@type": "xsd:decimal"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:premiseText": "(a) it is not practicable to examine or process the things at the warrant premises; or (b) the occupier of the premises consents in writing;",
      "cml:conclusionText": "The use of equipment to examine or process things is permitted if the occupier consents."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-57-ss-2-logic-alleninterval-2",
      "@type": "cml:LogicApplication",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:AllenInterval"
      },
      "cml:operator": "Overlaps",
      "cml:logicSummary": "Temporal overlap between consent and examination",
      "cml:executionSurface": "modalities::interval_reasoning",
      "cml:confidence": {
        "@value": 1.0,
        "@type": "xsd:decimal"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:premiseText": "(b) the occupier of the premises consents in writing;",
      "cml:conclusionText": "The temporal interval of 'consent' overlaps with the examination."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-57-ss-2-logic-ltl-3",
      "@type": "cml:LogicApplication",
      "cml:modality": {
        "@id": "cml:LTL"
      },
      "cml:operator": "Until",
      "cml:logicSummary": "Temporal relationship between consent and examination",
      "cml:executionSurface": "modalities::temporal_ltl",
      "cml:confidence": {
        "@value": 1.0,
        "@type": "xsd:decimal"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:premiseText": "(b) the occupier of the premises consents in writing;",
      "cml:conclusionText": "The examination occurs until the consent expires."
    },
    {
      "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-58",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "58 Use of electronic equipment at premises",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995#sec-58"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995.pdf, p.82",
      "values:originalText": "(1) The executing officer or an officer assisting may operate electronic\nequipment at the warrant premises to see whether evidential\nmaterial is accessible by doing so if he or she believes on\nreasonable grounds that the operation of the equipment can be\ncarried out without damage to the equipment.\n(2) If the executing officer or an officer assisting, after operating the\nequipment, finds that evidential material is accessible by doing so,\nhe or she may:\n(a) seize the equipment and any disk, tape or other associated\ndevice; or\n\n\nSearch, seizure and powers of arrest  Part 7\nProvisions relating to execution of search warrants  Division 2\nSection 58\nInternational War Crimes Tribunals Act 1995\nCompilation date: 28/03/2026\n(b) if the material can, by using facilities at the premises, be put\nin a documentary form-operate the facilities to put the\nmaterial in that form and seize the documents so produced; or\n(c) if the material can be transferred to a disk, tape or other\nstorage device:\n(i) that is brought to the premises; or\n(ii) that is at the premises and the use of which for the\npurpose has been agreed to in writing by the occupier of\nthe premises;\noperate the equipment or other facilities to copy the material\nto the storage device and take the storage device from the\npremises.\n(3) Equipment may be seized under paragraph (2)(a) only if:\n(a) it is not practicable to put the material in documentary form\nas mentioned in paragraph (2)(b) or to copy the material as\nmentioned in paragraph (2)(c); or\n(b) possession by the occupier of the equipment could constitute\nan offence against an Australian law.\n(4) If the executing officer or an officer assisting believes on\nreasonable grounds that:\n(a) evidential material may be accessible by operating electronic\nequipment at the warrant premises; and\n(b) expert assistance is required to operate the equipment; and\n(c) if he or she does not take action under this subsection, the\nmaterial may be destroyed, altered or otherwise interfered\nwith;\nhe or she may do whatever is necessary to secure the equipment,\nwhether by locking it up, placing a guard or otherwise.\n(5) The executing officer or an officer assisting must give notice to the\noccupier of the premises of his or her intention to secure equipment\nand of the fact that the equipment may be secured for up to 24\nhours.\n(6) The equipment may be secured:",
      "skos:definition": "(1) The executing officer or an officer assisting may operate electronic\nequipment at the warrant premises to see whether evidential\nmaterial is accessible by doing so if he or she believes on\nreasonable grounds that the operation of the equipment can be\ncarried out without damage to the equipment.\n(2) If the executing officer or an officer assisting, after operating the\nequipment, finds that evidential material is accessible by doing so,\nhe or she may:\n(a) seize the equipment and any disk, tape or other associated\ndevice; or\n\n\nSearch, seizure and powers of arrest  Part 7\nProvisions relating to execution of search warrants  Division 2\nSection 58\nInternational War Crimes Tribunals Act 1995\nCompilation date: 28/03/2026\n(b) if the material can, by using facilities at the premises, be put\nin a documentary form-operate the facilities to put the\nmaterial in that form and seize the documents so produced; or\n(c) if the material can be transferred to a disk, tape or other\nstorage device:\n(i) that is brought to the premises; or\n(ii) that is at the premises and the use of which for the\npurpose has been agreed to in writing by the occupier of\nthe premises;\noperate the equipment or other facilities to copy the material\nto the storage device and take the storage device from the\npremises.\n(3) Equipment may be seized under paragraph (2)(a) only if:\n(a) it is not practicable to put the material in documentary form\nas mentioned in paragraph (2)(b) or to copy the material as\nmentioned in paragraph (2)(c); or\n(b) possession by the occupier of the equipment could constitute\nan offence against an Australian law.\n(4) If the executing officer or an officer assisting believes on\nreasonable grounds that:\n(a) evidential material may be accessible by operating electronic\nequipment at the warrant premises; and\n(b) expert assistance is required to operate the equipment; and\n(c) if he or she does not take action under this subsection, the\nmaterial may be destroyed, altered or otherwise interfered",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-58-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-58-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-58-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-58-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-58-ss-5"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-58-ss-6"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "5bf96b66d5e3bbecfba043308bcc674e4c6bef5228a2ac65be373a40c25fef18"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995#sec-58",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "58 Use of electronic equipment at premises",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 82,
      "values:originalText": "(1) The executing officer or an officer assisting may operate electronic\nequipment at the warrant premises to see whether evidential\nmaterial is accessible by doing so if he or she believes on\nreasonable grounds that the operation of the equipment can be\ncarried out without damage to the equipment.\n(2) If the executing officer or an officer assisting, after operating the\nequipment, finds that evidential material is accessible by doing so,\nhe or she may:\n(a) seize the equipment and any disk, tape or other associated\ndevice; or\n\n\nSearch, seizure and powers of arrest  Part 7\nProvisions relating to execution of search warrants  Division 2\nSection 58\nInternational War Crimes Tribunals Act 1995\nCompilation date: 28/03/2026\n(b) if the material can, by using facilities at the premises, be put\nin a documentary form-operate the facilities to put the\nmaterial in that form and seize the documents so produced; or\n(c) if the material can be transferred to a disk, tape or other\nstorage device:\n(i) that is brought to the premises; or\n(ii) that is at the premises and the use of which for the\npurpose has been agreed to in writing by the occupier of\nthe premises;\noperate the equipment or other facilities to copy the material\nto the storage device and take the storage device from the\npremises.\n(3) Equipment may be seized under paragraph (2)(a) only if:\n(a) it is not practicable to put the material in documentary form\nas mentioned in paragraph (2)(b) or to copy the material as\nmentioned in paragraph (2)(c); or\n(b) possession by the occupier of the equipment could constitute\nan offence against an Australian law.\n(4) If the executing officer or an officer assisting believes on\nreasonable grounds that:\n(a) evidential material may be accessible by operating electronic\nequipment at the warrant premises; and\n(b) expert assistance is required to operate the equipment; and\n(c) if he or she does not take action under this subsection, the\nmaterial may be destroyed, altered or otherwise interfered\nwith;\nhe or she may do whatever is necessary to secure the equipment,\nwhether by locking it up, placing a guard or otherwise.\n(5) The executing officer or an officer assisting must give notice to the\noccupier of the premises of his or her intention to secure equipment\nand of the fact that the equipment may be secured for up to 24\nhours.\n(6) The equipment may be secured:"
    },
