    {
      "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-63",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "63 Searches without warrant in emergency situations",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995#sec-63"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995.pdf, p.87",
      "values:originalText": "(1) This section applies if a police officer suspects, on reasonable\ngrounds, that:\n(a) evidential material is in or on a conveyance; and\n(b) it is necessary to exercise a power under subsection (2) in\norder to prevent the material from being concealed, lost or\ndestroyed; and\n(c) it is necessary to exercise the power without the authority of\na search warrant because the circumstances are serious and\nurgent.\n(2) The police officer may:\n(a) stop and detain the conveyance; and\n(b) search the conveyance, and any container in or on the\nconveyance, for the material; and\n(c) seize the material if he or she finds it there.\n(3) If, in the course of searching for the material, the police officer\nfinds other evidential material or a thing relevant to an offence\nagainst an Australian law, the police officer may seize that material\nor thing if he or she suspects, on reasonable grounds, that:\n(a) it is necessary to seize it in order to prevent its concealment,\nloss or destruction; and\n(b) it is necessary to seize it without the authority of a search\nwarrant because the circumstances are serious and urgent.\n(4) The police officer must exercise his or her powers subject to\nsection 64.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) This section applies if a police officer suspects, on reasonable\ngrounds, that:\n(a) evidential material is in or on a conveyance; and\n(b) it is necessary to exercise a power under subsection (2) in\norder to prevent the material from being concealed, lost or\ndestroyed; and\n(c) it is necessary to exercise the power without the authority of\na search warrant because the circumstances are serious and\nurgent.\n(2) The police officer may:\n(a) stop and detain the conveyance; and\n(b) search the conveyance, and any container in or on the\nconveyance, for the material; and\n(c) seize the material if he or she finds it there.\n(3) If, in the course of searching for the material, the police officer\nfinds other evidential material or a thing relevant to an offence\nagainst an Australian law, the police officer may seize that material\nor thing if he or she suspects, on reasonable grounds, that:\n(a) it is necessary to seize it in order to prevent its concealment,\nloss or destruction; and\n(b) it is necessary to seize it without the authority of a search\nwarrant because the circumstances are serious and urgent.\n(4) The police officer must exercise his or her powers subject to\nsection 64.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-63-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-63-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-63-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-63-ss-4"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "bd614206580f010218225a731a984c31532f7a6db13f17f4cdf282167efee2a3"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995#sec-63",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "63 Searches without warrant in emergency situations",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 87,
      "values:originalText": "(1) This section applies if a police officer suspects, on reasonable\ngrounds, that:\n(a) evidential material is in or on a conveyance; and\n(b) it is necessary to exercise a power under subsection (2) in\norder to prevent the material from being concealed, lost or\ndestroyed; and\n(c) it is necessary to exercise the power without the authority of\na search warrant because the circumstances are serious and\nurgent.\n(2) The police officer may:\n(a) stop and detain the conveyance; and\n(b) search the conveyance, and any container in or on the\nconveyance, for the material; and\n(c) seize the material if he or she finds it there.\n(3) If, in the course of searching for the material, the police officer\nfinds other evidential material or a thing relevant to an offence\nagainst an Australian law, the police officer may seize that material\nor thing if he or she suspects, on reasonable grounds, that:\n(a) it is necessary to seize it in order to prevent its concealment,\nloss or destruction; and\n(b) it is necessary to seize it without the authority of a search\nwarrant because the circumstances are serious and urgent.\n(4) The police officer must exercise his or her powers subject to\nsection 64."
    },
    {
