    {
      "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-71",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "71 Power to conduct an ordinary search or strip search",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995#sec-71"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995.pdf, p.92",
      "values:originalText": "(1) If a person who has been arrested under this Act or pursuant to a\nwarrant issued under this Act is brought to a police station, a police\nofficer may:\n(a) if an ordinary search of the person has not been conducted—\nconduct an ordinary search of the person; or\n(b) subject to this section, conduct a strip search of the person.\n(2) A strip search may be conducted if:\n(a) a police officer suspects on reasonable grounds that:\n(i) the person has in his or her possession evidential\nmaterial relating to the Tribunal offence to which the\nperson’s custody relates; or\n(ii) the person has in his or her possession a seizable item;\nor\n(iii) a visual inspection of the person’s body will provide\nevidence of the person’s involvement in that offence;\nand\n(b) the police officer suspects on reasonable grounds that it is\nnecessary to conduct a strip search of the person in order to\nrecover that thing or to discover that evidence; and\n(c) a police officer of the rank of superintendent or higher has\napproved the conduct of the search.\n(3) Subject to section 72, a strip search may also be conducted if the\nperson consents in writing.\n(4) Subject to section 72, a strip search may be conducted in the\npresence of a medical practitioner who may assist in the search.\n(5) The approval may be obtained by telephone, telex, fax or other\nelectronic means.\n(6) A police officer who gives or refuses to give an approval for the\npurposes of paragraph (2)(c) must make a record of the decision\nand of the reasons for the decision.\n\n\nSearch, seizure and powers of arrest  Part 7\nArrest and related matters  Division 4\nSection 72\nInternational War Crimes Tribunals Act 1995\nCompilation date: 28/03/2026\n(7) Such force as is necessary and reasonable in the circumstances may\nbe used to conduct a strip search under subsection (2).\n(8) Any item of a kind referred to in subparagraph (2)(a)(i) or (ii) that\nis found during a strip search may be seized.",
      "skos:definition": "(1) If a person who has been arrested under this Act or pursuant to a\nwarrant issued under this Act is brought to a police station, a police\nofficer may:\n(a) if an ordinary search of the person has not been conducted—\nconduct an ordinary search of the person; or\n(b) subject to this section, conduct a strip search of the person.\n(2) A strip search may be conducted if:\n(a) a police officer suspects on reasonable grounds that:\n(i) the person has in his or her possession evidential\nmaterial relating to the Tribunal offence to which the\nperson’s custody relates; or\n(ii) the person has in his or her possession a seizable item;\nor\n(iii) a visual inspection of the person’s body will provide\nevidence of the person’s involvement in that offence;\nand\n(b) the police officer suspects on reasonable grounds that it is\nnecessary to conduct a strip search of the person in order to\nrecover that thing or to discover that evidence; and\n(c) a police officer of the rank of superintendent or higher has\napproved the conduct of the search.\n(3) Subject to section 72, a strip search may also be conducted if the\nperson consents in writing.\n(4) Subject to section 72, a strip search may be conducted in the\npresence of a medical practitioner who may assist in the search.\n(5) The approval may be obtained by telephone, telex, fax or other\nelectronic means.\n(6) A police officer who gives or refuses to give an approval for the\npurposes of paragraph (2)(c) must make a record of the decision\nand of the reasons for the decision.\n\n\nSearch, seizure and powers of arrest  Part 7\nArrest and related matters  Division 4\nSection 72\nInternational War Crimes Tribunals Act 1995\nCompilation date: 28/03/2026\n(7) Such force as is necessary and reasonable in the circumstances may\nbe used to conduct a strip search under subsection (2).\n(8) Any item of a kind referred to in subparagraph (2)(a)(i) or (ii) that\nis found during a strip search may be seized.",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-71-ss-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-71-ss-2"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-71-ss-3"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-71-ss-4"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-71-ss-5"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-71-ss-6"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-71-ss-7"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995-sec-71-ss-8"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "1cbb83706893a0e89777be78f88e9c36dc550d7160d21329b8c4679454f3559b"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/international-war-crimes-tribunals-act-1995#sec-71",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "71 Power to conduct an ordinary search or strip search",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 92,
      "values:originalText": "(1) If a person who has been arrested under this Act or pursuant to a\nwarrant issued under this Act is brought to a police station, a police\nofficer may:\n(a) if an ordinary search of the person has not been conducted—\nconduct an ordinary search of the person; or\n(b) subject to this section, conduct a strip search of the person.\n(2) A strip search may be conducted if:\n(a) a police officer suspects on reasonable grounds that:\n(i) the person has in his or her possession evidential\nmaterial relating to the Tribunal offence to which the\nperson’s custody relates; or\n(ii) the person has in his or her possession a seizable item;\nor\n(iii) a visual inspection of the person’s body will provide\nevidence of the person’s involvement in that offence;\nand\n(b) the police officer suspects on reasonable grounds that it is\nnecessary to conduct a strip search of the person in order to\nrecover that thing or to discover that evidence; and\n(c) a police officer of the rank of superintendent or higher has\napproved the conduct of the search.\n(3) Subject to section 72, a strip search may also be conducted if the\nperson consents in writing.\n(4) Subject to section 72, a strip search may be conducted in the\npresence of a medical practitioner who may assist in the search.\n(5) The approval may be obtained by telephone, telex, fax or other\nelectronic means.\n(6) A police officer who gives or refuses to give an approval for the\npurposes of paragraph (2)(c) must make a record of the decision\nand of the reasons for the decision.\n\n\nSearch, seizure and powers of arrest  Part 7\nArrest and related matters  Division 4\nSection 72\nInternational War Crimes Tribunals Act 1995\nCompilation date: 28/03/2026\n(7) Such force as is necessary and reasonable in the circumstances may\nbe used to conduct a strip search under subsection (2).\n(8) Any item of a kind referred to in subparagraph (2)(a)(i) or (ii) that\nis found during a strip search may be seized."
    },
